From 6b95dc2e8bf27dd7cda7b2e0ae5063ec0a42161d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:55:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Check the answer's figures against the records they came
 from
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The system prompt already tells the model to ground every claim in retrieved
data and to say "I don't have that" rather than fill a gap. That is an
instruction, and an instruction is a hope. Nothing in the service checked
whether the model complied, so "the assistant only answers from your data" was
a claim we made on the model's behalf without evidence.

verifyGrounding() closes that. After the model answers, every figure in the
text is looked for in the data the tools actually returned, and graded:

  in-source    the figure is in the retrieved payload
  derived      it is a sum or difference of two figures that are
               ("down 3.6 kg since January" is arithmetic, not invention)
  unverified   it is neither

Only the unverified ones travel to the caller, on both routes and in the
per-turn log — so grounding becomes a rate you can watch move rather than an
assurance, and a model swap that starts inventing figures shows up before a
trainer has to notice it.

Two decisions worth stating plainly, since both are limits:

It checks NUMBERS, not prose. "Rahul is progressing well" is unfalsifiable
here and always will be. The figures are what a trainer acts on — a weight, a
session count, an expiry date, a balance — and a fabricated number is a wrong
decision about a real person in a way a fabricated adjective is not.

Small integers match by coincidence, so a hallucinated "3 sessions" will
usually be graded in-source. That is a real false negative and no threshold
fixes it — raising one would start hiding genuine findings instead. The
distinctive figures are where this earns its keep, and they are also the ones
that do damage when wrong.

The check runs against the ERP payload, never the prompt. Mutation testing
found that one: verifying against the conversation lets the model launder a
figure by agreeing with a trainer's leading question ("his body fat is 22.7%,
right?") — the one case a trainer most needs flagged, since they cannot catch
it by reading. Seven mutants, seven caught, including "always report clean".
